  <Document>
    <name>🌫️ LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 6:12AM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 7:12AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (derived HRRR analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/fog/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261006-t09z-fog-anl" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Fog-risk index 0-100 (DERIVED from HRRR analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> green low risk &rarr; yellow elevated &rarr; orange high &rarr; red very high &rarr; <b>ACTIVE FOG 85+</b> red-violet into deep purple dense active fog (visibility-confirmed) to <b>HIGHEST+ 100</b>. Same index always shows the same color. Thermodynamic risk with a visibility confirmation gate — not a copy of the visibility layer.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km analysis, hourly cycles: TMP+DPT+RH (2 m) + UGRD/VGRD (10 m) + VIS surface (derived fog-risk index with active-fog visibility gate)<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 6:12AM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 7:12AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</h2><p>A derived fog-risk index from operational HRRR analysis: low greens mark clear air, yellows and oranges mark elevated fog risk from saturated calm air, and reds into deep purple mark active fog confirmed by collapsed visibility. Built for Great Lakes navigation, shoreline conditions, aviation awareness, and nighttime environmental monitoring. Derivation: base = 100*rh_factor*spread_factor*calm_factor; rh_factor=clip((RH-70)/30,0,1); spread_factor=clip((3-S)/3,0,1), S=TMP-DPT C deg; calm_factor=clip((6-wspd)/6,0.35,1). Visibility gate only raises: VIS<1000m & RH>=95 -> >=85 (ACTIVE FOG); 1000<=VIS<5000m -> >=60. Distinct from raw visibility: calm saturated air scores high even before visibility collapses, and observed fog confirms what thermodynamics understate.</p><p>Fog science (boundary-layer meteorology and aerosol microphysics) studies saturation, radiative cooling, and droplet nucleation that reduce visibility below one kilometer. Radiation fog forms over land and calm water on clear nights; advection and steam fog form where air-water temperature contrasts drive lake-effect events. The index separates fog likelihood from raw visibility so developing risk reads before, and active fog reads during, an event.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/fog/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261006-t09z-fog-anl" width="600" alt="key"><br>Fog-risk index 0-100 (DERIVED from HRRR analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> green low risk &rarr; yellow elevated &rarr; orange high &rarr; red very high &rarr; <b>ACTIVE FOG 85+</b> red-violet into deep purple dense active fog (visibility-confirmed) to <b>HIGHEST+ 100</b>. Same index always shows the same color. Thermodynamic risk with a visibility confirmation gate — not a copy of the visibility layer.<br><b>Units:</b> fog-risk index 0-100 (display; derived, see derivation)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km analysis, hourly cycles: TMP+DPT+RH (2 m) + UGRD/VGRD (10 m) + VIS surface (derived fog-risk index with active-fog visibility gate)<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
